title What generalizing bought, priced on the third family
prompt Keaton -- kg
think Took tools/m, 87 unheard, of which 69 are Mycelium -- this tree's own .myc notation. Ran all 81 matching the prefix before building anything; every one green.
think The third family cost a file of comments, six assert lines calling the shared script, and one roster row. No scan of its own, no control of its own. That is the price of having generalized the second choir rather than copying the first, and it is worth recording as a number rather than a feeling.
obs Three families and 212 witnesses now run under three roster rows, and unreached falls 898 to 818.
obs THE FAULT WORTH KEEPING: I seeded the file by copying its sibling, and a sed left the ELDER family's name in four places -- including the GREEN claim line and two error messages. The witness ran green the whole time and announced that every HUNK witness had been sung, while singing Mycelium.
obs Nothing in the tree would have caught that. A claim line is prose inside a program, and no guard reads it for truth; the run was correct and the sentence describing the run was false. Caught by reading the file, which is the only thing that catches it.
obs That is the same shape as the stale assertions found two laps ago, arriving from the opposite direction: there, a guard's words stayed true while the tree moved; here, the run moved while the guard's words stayed. Both are a program and its description drifting apart, and only a person notices.
